WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.740
Dangerous driving, falling for the&nbsp;
wrong person, and dancing with cartoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.660
birds. These movies really tested the&nbsp;
limits of how much an actor could take!

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:16.260
The dark, claustrophobic sets and constantly&nbsp;
gushing water made for some difficult days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.840
on the set of James Cameron's 1989 sci-fi&nbsp;
flick The Abyss. But one day stands out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.880
Mary Elizabeth Mastrantonio. While filming a&nbsp;
scene in which Ed Harris performs CPR on her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.560
Cameron directed Harris to actually&nbsp;
slap Mastrantonio. He had to repeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.440
the slap when the take was&nbsp;
obstructed by Harris' arm.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.740
After the next take was botched&nbsp;
when the camera ran out of film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.600
Mastrantonio actually got up and left&nbsp;
the set. She stayed away for hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.576
leaving Harris to practice with a sandbag until&nbsp;
she was ready to finish filming the scene.

00:00:48.576 --> 00:00:52.500
Captain America: Civil War focuses&nbsp;
on the rift between the Avengers as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.400
the U.S. government tries to limit&nbsp;
their reach. This new oversight is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.600
partially inspired by the exploits of&nbsp;
the Winter Soldier, aka Bucky Barnes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.496
who is being pursued by various factions. In an&nbsp;
attempt to keep his old friend out of harm's way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.496 --> 00:01:09.816
Steve Rogers — played by Chris Evans —&nbsp;
tries to get to him before anyone else can.

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:14.220
In a dramatic chase scene, Bucky attempts&nbsp;
to escape by flying away in a helicopter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.920
This prompts Rogers to try to keep the powerful&nbsp;
vehicle grounded with his own two hands. In an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.780
impressive feat of strength, he actually manages&nbsp;
to bring the helicopter crashing down. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.780
filming this scene was so intense that it left him&nbsp;
injured. Speaking to ET Online, Evans remarked,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.660
"And I actually messed up my&nbsp;
arm doing it, I really did."

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.380
According to Evans, this stunt wasn't&nbsp;
created through special effects:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.220
He's truly straining to keep this&nbsp;
helicopter under control. He explained,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.140
"It's a fake helicopter, obviously, but it&nbsp;
was drifting beyond the ability of control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.040
I tweaked a little something. To&nbsp;
this day, I'm still messed up."

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.968
While on the set of his 2002 thriller Panic Room,&nbsp;
director David Fincher told The New York Times,

00:01:55.968 --> 00:01:59.568
"I felt like I needed to be more&nbsp;
attentive to watching the actors."

00:01:59.568 --> 00:02:04.380
He put that theory into motion for his next&nbsp;
film, Zodiac — a period piece about the search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.120
for the "Zodiac Killer" who murdered their&nbsp;
way through Northern California in the 1970s.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.600
Jake Gyllenhaal, who starred as&nbsp;
cartoonist-turned-crime reporter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.740
Robert Graysmith, bore the brunt of Fincher's&nbsp;
commitment to putting his actors through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.880
wringer. According to Gyllenhaal, for&nbsp;
one scene, Fincher required 70 takes;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.800
for another, he asked for 90. Plus,&nbsp;
because the film was shot digitally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:31.284
Fincher could spontaneously delete a slew&nbsp;
of takes all at once. Gyllenhaal said,

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:34.944
"But there is a stopping point.&nbsp;
There's a point at which you go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.400
'That's what we have to work with.'&nbsp;
But we would reshoot things."

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:45.120
Although it was far from her first major role,&nbsp;
Kate Winslet's performance in Titanic launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.220
her to worldwide stardom, but filming the&nbsp;
movie was not a pleasant experience. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.500
Telegraph reports that there were multiple&nbsp;
issues on set that caused both the cast and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.860
crew to question whether they wanted&nbsp;
to remain part of the project at all.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.240
"But this ship can't sink."
"She's made of iron sir. I assure you, she can."

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:08.016
One particularly troubling moment came&nbsp;
when Winslet filmed a scene in the water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.016 --> 00:03:13.620
According to The Times, she genuinely feared&nbsp;
she might drown in the set's gargantuan water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.780
tank. Things were so rough, she even&nbsp;
expressed a desire to steer clear of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.764
director James Cameron in the future. As&nbsp;
reported by The Guardian, Winslet said,

00:03:22.764 --> 00:03:26.100
"He has a temper like you wouldn't&nbsp;
believe. [...] You'd have to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.004
me a lot of money to work with Jim again."

00:03:29.004 --> 00:03:35.124
Years later, however, she softened her stance:&nbsp;
The pair collaborated on Avatar: The Way of Water.

00:03:35.124 --> 00:03:40.980
Quentin Tarantino's "Kill Bill" movies follow&nbsp;
Beatrix Kiddo, aka the Bride, as she seeks revenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:46.320
on the Deadly Viper Assassination Squad. Years&nbsp;
earlier, the group betrayed her by killing her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.548
groom-to-be and putting her in a coma. Now she's&nbsp;
back on her feet and ready to dispense justice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.548 --> 00:03:57.540
Uma Thurman shines as the deadly Kiddo, earning&nbsp;
international acclaim for her performance.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.640
In the second movie, a scene in which Kiddo&nbsp;
drives a car with steely determination proved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:06.180
to be problematic. As The New&nbsp;
York Times eventually revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:11.700
Tarantino pushed Thurman to drive the vehicle&nbsp;
herself, despite her well-founded concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.040
that it would be unsafe. Those fears were&nbsp;
soon justified when the car hit a tree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.860
causing serious injuries that Thurman still deals&nbsp;
with to this day. She revealed that immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.600
after the incident, she was scared she would&nbsp;
be unable to walk again. Thurman remarked,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.340
"I went from being a creative contributor&nbsp;
and performer to being like a broken tool."

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:36.120
After a 15-year battle to&nbsp;
get footage of the crash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.500
Tarantino finally surrendered it to&nbsp;
Thurman. But, as Thurman herself noted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.868
this can't undo the damage that was&nbsp;
done, or the pain that still lingers.

00:04:44.868 --> 00:04:50.700
In the 1993 film What's Eating Gilbert Grape?,&nbsp;
Johnny Depp starred as the titular Gilbert — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.660
guy stuck in a small town caring for his&nbsp;
developmentally disabled younger brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.660 --> 00:05:00.540
and their housebound mother. Cast as the mom was&nbsp;
Darlene Cates, an acting novice that director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:05.664
Lasse Hallström spotted on The Sally Jessy Raphael&nbsp;
Show discussing her own struggle with obesity.

00:05:05.664 --> 00:05:10.740
Depp was going through some personal stuff during&nbsp;
the film's shoot and was using alcohol to cope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.852
Hallström later told Entertainment Weekly that he&nbsp;
also empathized with his character a bit too much:

00:05:15.852 --> 00:05:18.060
"It was more that he could relate to the troubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:21.696
family life and the desire to get&nbsp;
out of a small-town situation."

00:05:21.696 --> 00:05:26.220
Depp was in such a sensitive place that&nbsp;
he had trouble filming scenes where his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:30.360
character had to make disparaging comments&nbsp;
about his mother. Cates recalled that each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.860
night after those scenes were shot, Depp&nbsp;
called her to apologize. She explained,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.020
"Here he is the professional; he's done all this&nbsp;
stuff before. But I thought, 'What a tribute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.019
to his kindness that he would take the time to&nbsp;
consider that I might be hurt by what he said.'"

00:05:44.427 --> 00:05:48.528
"You're my knight in shimmering&nbsp;
armor. Did you know that?"

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.220
To do his job killing bad guys&nbsp;
and foiling terrorist plots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.740
007 needs to use a firearm on occasion.

00:05:55.956 --> 00:06:00.876
*guns firing*

00:06:00.876 --> 00:06:09.768
One of the actors who portrayed Bond onscreen&nbsp;
had a really hard time with those moments.

00:06:09.768 --> 00:06:15.240
Roger Moore starred in seven 007 movies,&nbsp;
including The Spy Who Loved Me, Moonraker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.920
and, ironically, The Man with the Golden Gun.&nbsp;
While he doesn't regret playing James Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.440
according to the BBC, Moore once said,

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:26.280
"I regret that sadly heroes in general&nbsp;
are depicted with guns in their hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:30.276
and to tell the truth I have always&nbsp;
hated guns and what they represent."

00:06:30.276 --> 00:06:35.940
It all goes back to his childhood. In his memoir&nbsp;
"My Word is My Bond," Moore said that as a boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.740
he was rendered deaf for a few days when a&nbsp;
gun "blew up" in his hands. A few years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:46.152
a friend shot a BB gun into the future star's&nbsp;
leg. When he'd have to use a firearm onscreen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.152 --> 00:06:50.220
Moore would squeeze his eyes tightly in&nbsp;
discomfort as he pulled the trigger on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.660
his prop Walther PPK firearm. By the&nbsp;
time he filmed his final Bond movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:59.280
he publicly lamented that the film&nbsp;
series had, quote, "turned so violent."

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.520
In Quentin Tarantino's Django Unchained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:08.160
Jamie Foxx plays Django Freeman — a formerly&nbsp;
enslaved man who joins Dr. King Schultz to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.512
rescue his wife from fearsome plantation owner&nbsp;
Calvin J. Candie, played by Leonardo DiCaprio.

00:07:13.512 --> 00:07:18.720
As you would expect from a Tarantino movie,&nbsp;
"Django Unchained" contains lots of stylized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.700
violence, gory fight scenes, and plenty&nbsp;
of foul language, including a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:28.860
racist slurs. DiCaprio took issue with his&nbsp;
dialogue, which required him to use these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:34.980
ugly terms multiple times. As Metro.co.uk&nbsp;
revealed, the actor needed outside advice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:40.740
move forward with the brutal script: Both Foxx&nbsp;
and Samuel L. Jackson urged him to perform the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.940
dialogue as it was written for the sake of&nbsp;
authenticity. Though he did manage to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:50.160
forward, DiCaprio said that he found playing&nbsp;
Candie incredibly tough. The actor remarked,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.120
"It was this incredibly interesting&nbsp;
horrific character. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.080
there was absolutely nothing about&nbsp;
this man I could identify with."

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:04.620
The psychological horror film Black Swan is&nbsp;
memorable for many reasons. It was a huge box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:10.560
office hit, earning more than $300 million&nbsp;
against its budget of $13 million. It also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.708
won a vast array of awards, including&nbsp;
five nominations from the 2011 Oscars.

00:08:15.708 --> 00:08:19.008
Natalie Portman stars as promising&nbsp;
ballet dancer Nina Sayers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.008 --> 00:08:22.800
who struggles to cope with the demands&nbsp;
of portraying the dual role of Odette&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:28.020
and Odile in Tchaikovsky's "Swan Lake." She&nbsp;
soon forms a rivalry with fellow dancer Lily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.992
played by Mila Kunis, and slowly&nbsp;
begins to lose touch with reality.

00:08:31.992 --> 00:08:37.020
At one point in the film, Kunis and Portman's&nbsp;
characters lock themselves in a room and end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:41.580
up in bed together — a scene that was something&nbsp;
of a trial for both actresses. In an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:46.020
with Parade, Kunis revealed that she and&nbsp;
Portman knew the scene would be a hot topic.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.980
"It wasn't done for shock value, but we&nbsp;
weren't looking forward to it. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.340
think we were laughing between takes.&nbsp;
I think we were like, 'Let's just get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:57.528
this over with.' It's uncomfortable&nbsp;
doing a sex scene. It's just bizarre."

00:08:57.528 --> 00:09:01.008
Portman confirmed this in a&nbsp;
conversation with MTV News:

00:09:01.008 --> 00:09:03.600
"It was pretty awkward. I&nbsp;
almost feel like it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.060
be easier to do it with someone you didn't know."

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:11.220
That said, both Portman and Kunis agreed that&nbsp;
their friendship made the whole process smoother.

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:17.940
Before Horrible Bosses, there was 9 to 5.&nbsp;
This dark comedy from 1980 centered on three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:24.120
overworked, overlooked female secretaries enacting&nbsp;
brutal revenge on a chauvinistic pig of a manager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:28.440
played by Dabney Coleman. Dolly Parton, Lily&nbsp;
Tomlin, and Jane Fonda headed up the cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:33.756
with Fonda also producing. But a mere week&nbsp;
after filming began, Tomlin tried to quit.

00:09:33.756 --> 00:09:39.300
What set off the legendary comic actress?&nbsp;
She watched the raw footage of herself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:43.380
was embarrassed by her performance&nbsp;
— particularly the scenes for a Snow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:46.620
White-inspired animated fantasy&nbsp;
sequence where she acted opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:51.120
a yet-to-be-created cartoon bird. On The Late&nbsp;
Show with Stephen Colbert, Tomlin explained,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.712
"And I was talking to the&nbsp;
birds that weren't there…"

00:09:53.712 --> 00:09:54.312
"Oh yeah?"

00:09:54.312 --> 00:09:59.580
"And I was not doing a good job. And I thought,&nbsp;
'Oh this is, I'm going to be horrible in this.'"

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:05.400
Despite her reservations, she stuck with&nbsp;
the film, and an '80s classic was born.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:10.920
During production of the first Fast and Furious&nbsp;
movie, series regular and all-around badass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.540
Michelle Rodriguez actually considered walking&nbsp;
away over concerns with the way her character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:20.520
Letty Ortiz, was written. Specifically, Rodriguez&nbsp;
took issue with the way the script called for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:27.240
Letty to betray her true love Dom and fall for&nbsp;
undercover cop Brian. She felt this betrayal made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:32.700
no sense for a woman who clearly preferred,&nbsp;
in her words, "the alpha-est of alpha males"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.680
to a pretty boy. Refusing to compromise her&nbsp;
vision of Letty, Rodriguez put her foot down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:42.540
telling producers she'd quit the movie if she&nbsp;
had to. Fortunately for Fast and Furious fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:48.156
the storyline was tweaked to Rodriguez's&nbsp;
liking, and the rest is blockbuster history.
 &nbsp;

00:10:49.296 --> 00:10:53.100
Starring in one of the most anticipated&nbsp;
movies of the century is an opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:57.660
most actors would kill for. But the&nbsp;
pressure of filming 2015's Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:02.760
The Force Awakens nearly proved too much for Daisy&nbsp;
Ridley. The British newcomer didn't have much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:07.800
experience before J.J. Abrams cast her as Rey,&nbsp;
and Screen Rant reports she almost quit on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:13.860
first day when he told her that her performance&nbsp;
during a desert scene felt "wooden." That note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:18.300
along with bearing the weight of the biggest&nbsp;
franchise in movie history, almost prompted Ridley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:21.722
to get on a spaceship to anywhere far, far away.

00:11:21.722 --> 00:11:24.285
Shelley Duvall set a Guinness World Record for her 

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:29.220
portrayal of Wendy in Stanley Kubrick's 1980&nbsp;
horror classic The Shining – but it's not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:35.400
record anyone would want. The perfectionist&nbsp;
Kubrick insisted on 127 takes of the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:41.040
where Duvall's hysterical character uses a bat to&nbsp;
fend off a deranged Jack Nicholson. By the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.780
she so was exhausted and dehydrated from&nbsp;
crying that she nearly quit. She didn't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:51.132
but the stress of filming the scene was bad&nbsp;
enough that she reportedly began losing her hair.
 &nbsp;

00:11:51.132 --> 00:11:54.780
Before she landed leading roles in huge&nbsp;
blockbusters like Avatar and Guardians of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.780 --> 00:12:00.360
the Galaxy, Saldana was just another struggling&nbsp;
actress getting by on bit parts. She had a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.380
one as Anamaria in 2003's Pirates of the&nbsp;
Caribbean: The Curse of the Black Pearl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:08.700
and she said she was treated so badly that she&nbsp;
nearly quit, telling The Hollywood Reporter,

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:11.700
"It was very elitist. People&nbsp;
disrespecting me because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:15.144
look at my number on a call sheet&nbsp;
and they think I'm not important."

00:12:15.144 --> 00:12:19.944
It was so demoralizing, Saldana said she&nbsp;
even considered quitting acting altogether.
 &nbsp;

00:12:19.944 --> 00:12:23.160
Mike Myers created the character of&nbsp;
Wayne Campbell on Saturday Night Live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:27.816
and the recurring sketch became so popular&nbsp;
that Paramount wanted a Wayne's World movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.816 --> 00:12:31.020
Myers wrote the screenplay, and he was&nbsp;
especially excited about the scene where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:35.616
Wayne and the boys pack into the Mirthmobile&nbsp;
and headbang to Queen's "Bohemian Rhapsody."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.616 --> 00:12:39.000
But the studio wanted to replace the&nbsp;
song with something fresher and cheaper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:43.968
to license. Myers thought the Queen song was&nbsp;
crucial, as he revealed on WTF with Marc Maron.

00:12:43.968 --> 00:12:46.727
Both Myers and Bohemian Rhapsody stayed.

00:12:46.727 --> 00:12:48.921
Gene Hackman won an Oscar for his turn as New 

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:54.660
York City police detective Jimmy "Popeye" Doyle&nbsp;
in 1971's The French Connection, but it almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:59.580
didn't happen. A notoriously difficult actor,&nbsp;
he often clashed with director William Friedkin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:04.740
especially after Friedkin revealed Paul Newman&nbsp;
was his first choice for the part. Things came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:09.720
a head during a scene where Hackman – dressed in&nbsp;
a Santa Claus costume – tackles a criminal on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:16.080
street. Friedkin demanded 27 takes, and Hackman&nbsp;
nearly quit. Fortunately, he stayed and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:19.480
mentioned it in his speech at the Academy Awards.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:22.341
Few wrestling stars are bigger than John Cena, 

00:13:22.341 --> 00:13:27.720
so when WWE produced the direct to video cop&nbsp;
thriller 12 Rounds in 2009, casting him as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:32.580
the lead was a no brainer. Although his day&nbsp;
job is to battle hulking beasts in the ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:38.040
Cena apparently isn't fearless. One scene called&nbsp;
for his character to rappel down a skyscraper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:43.020
and dangle mid-air from a rope. But Cena&nbsp;
revealed later that his severe fear of heights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:47.880
triggered intense anxiety and he considered&nbsp;
walking away before he ever got up there.
 &nbsp;

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:53.040
A big part of the fourth Rocky movie&nbsp;
involves Rocky's mentor, Apollo Creed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:57.900
staging a comeback. Played by former NFL&nbsp;
linebacker Carl Weathers, Creed gets roughed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:03.000
up in the ring pretty bad by Communist pugilist&nbsp;
Ivan Drago, played by Dolph Lundgren. But one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:07.020
of the boxing scenes got a little too real&nbsp;
when Lundgren threw Weathers into a corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:12.480
causing Weathers to break character and throw a&nbsp;
real punch. Lundgren responded in kind, knocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:17.820
Weathers out. When he regained consciousness, he&nbsp;
quit the movie and sent production into limbo for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:22.092
four days. That's when Sylvester Stallone&nbsp;
finally convinced Weathers to return.
 &nbsp;

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:28.500
Alfred Hitchcock had a reputation for being unkind&nbsp;
to actresses, but Tippi Hedren signed on to 1963's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:34.320
The Birds under the guarantee that she'd only be&nbsp;
attacked with mechanical birds during the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:39.360
climactic scene. But Hitchcock is the king of&nbsp;
twists, and he wanted Hedren's terror to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:45.540
real, so he instructed crew members to throw&nbsp;
real, live birds at her. This real-life game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:52.800
of Angry Birds went on for five full days and&nbsp;
Hedren had a breakdown when it was over. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:57.540
only finished the movie when a doctor ordered&nbsp;
Hitchcock to give her a week off to recover.
 &nbsp;

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:01.620
The Walking Dead nearly suffered what would&nbsp;
have been a devastating loss in Season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:06.900
when Lauren Cohan, who plays Maggie, contemplated&nbsp;
quitting over a particularly stressful scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:11.640
After reading the script and realizing Maggie&nbsp;
was supposed to perform an emergency c-section&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:16.800
on Lori Grimes — while the character's son looked&nbsp;
on — she took her concerns to co-star Steven Yeun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:21.660
explaining she was worried it would be too&nbsp;
intense. But after further consideration, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:27.180
exactly why she decided to stay. Recognizing that&nbsp;
The Walking Dead is a show about people pushed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:32.520
grueling extremes, she drew on her discomfort&nbsp;
to deliver an affectingly honest performance.
 &nbsp;

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:37.320
You think you hated Fox's first couple of&nbsp;
Fantastic Four movies? Try being Jessica Alba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:41.880
whose experiences playing Sue "Invisible&nbsp;
Woman" Storm nearly made her walk away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:47.100
acting altogether. Alba's frustrations stemmed&nbsp;
from working with director Tim Story, who she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:51.780
later claimed issued absurd demands like telling&nbsp;
her to keep her facial expression flat during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:58.020
crying scene so she'd stay "pretty." After 2007's&nbsp;
Rise of the Silver Surfer, she realized she didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:02.640
care about the movie business anymore, and has&nbsp;
been more selective about acting projects. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:06.540
things have still worked out pretty well for her&nbsp;
— she went on to co-found the Honest Company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:10.082
currently valued around $1 billion.

00:16:10.082 --> 00:16:12.189
Interview with the Vampire united Tom 

00:16:12.189 --> 00:16:17.304
Cruise and Brad Pitt in a visually sumptuous&nbsp;
adaptation of the bestselling Anne Rice novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.304 --> 00:16:22.500
but being part of 1994's most eagerly anticipated&nbsp;
beefcake summit wasn't all it was cracked up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:28.080
be for Pitt. Years after the movie came and went,&nbsp;
Pitt admitted he was so miserable during filming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:33.420
that he actually approached producer David Geffen&nbsp;
about buying out his contract — only to reconsider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:37.163
after Geffen informed him it'd cost $40 million.

00:16:37.163 --> 00:16:39.669
It's hard to imagine Gandalf being played by 

00:16:39.669 --> 00:16:44.820
anyone other than Sir Ian McKellen, who played&nbsp;
the wizard in all six Lord of the Rings and Hobbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:48.540
films. But if things had worked out a little&nbsp;
differently during the filming of The Hobbit:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:52.800
An Unexpected Journey, director Peter Jackson&nbsp;
might've had to find a different actor. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:57.540
because McKellen was acting almost exclusively&nbsp;
to a green, screen since his character towered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:02.100
over the hobbits and their interactions had to&nbsp;
be stitched together digitally. The frustration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:07.740
of acting alone eventually got to McKellen, who&nbsp;
broke down on the set at one point. Jackson did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:11.700
a little emergency outreach to convince&nbsp;
him to stick around, and the crew held a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:16.272
"Gandalf Appreciation Day," and decorated&nbsp;
his tent with Lord of the Rings mementos.

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:21.660
Marvel's The Avengers was such a huge, ambitious&nbsp;
project that it attracted actors who usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:26.820
don't do comic book movies, including two-time&nbsp;
Oscar nominee Jeremy Renner as Clint Barton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.820 --> 00:17:31.920
a.k.a. Hawkeye. While some of the more famous&nbsp;
heroes in the movie — like Iron Man, the Hulk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:36.480
Captain America — got to do more interesting&nbsp;
things, Hawkeye spent most of The Avengers as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:43.140
the brainwashed servant of villain Loki. Renner&nbsp;
was...displeased. He explained during a Q&amp;A event:

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:45.120
"I was just getting to know who Hawkeye was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:49.260
and then zap, I go round like a&nbsp;
zombie. I'm like Loki's minion."

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:54.840
But don't worry, Renner took it like a champ.&nbsp;
Haha, just kidding! He sulked and whined about it.

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:57.180
He later recalled that in each&nbsp;
scene, he'd make horrible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.180 --> 00:18:00.792
audible grunts of pain. When&nbsp;
asked why by the crew, he said:

00:18:00.792 --> 00:18:04.860
"I'm giving you an option, if you just want&nbsp;
to kick me out of this movie. Just you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:09.408
at any given moment, if you wanna kill me&nbsp;
off, daddy’s gonna be having a heart attack."

